{"id":"bbc7bf40-fb23-4935-a44f-c3d7bb6bc386","object":"chat.completion","created":1785018854,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case.get('nodes')\\n    if nodes is None:\\n        raise ValueError('Missing nodes')\\n    required_symbols = case.get('required_symbols')\\n    if required_symbols is None or not isinstance(required_symbols, list):\\n        raise ValueError('Missing or invalid required_symbols')\\n    required = set(required_symbols)\\n    node_by_id = {}\\n    children_of = {}\\n    symbol_counts = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        nid = n.get('id')\\n        if nid is None:\\n            raise ValueError('Node missing id')\\n        parent = n.get('parent')\\n        symbol = n.get('symbol')\\n        depth = n.get('depth')\\n        removable = n.get('removable')\\n        if symbol is None or depth is None or removable is None:\\n            raise ValueError('Node missing required field')\\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_of.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[symbol] = symbol_counts.get(symbol, 0) + 1\\n    for sym in required:\\n        if symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) == 0:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Required symbol {sym!r} not present')\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if n['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if n['symbol'] in required:\\n            continue\\n        child_ids = children_of.get(nid, [])\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids):\\n            continue\\n        sym = n['symbol']\\n        new_count = symbol_counts[sym] - 1\\n        if new_count < 1 and sym in required:\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n        symbol_counts[sym] = new_count\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. The problem: \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" - given a tree of nodes with symbols, parent, removable flag, utility, depth, and required_symbols list, we need to remove some removable nodes while preserving that all required symbols are still present in the tree (i.e., at least one node of each required symbol remains). We must consider deeper nodes before shallower nodes (i.e., process nodes in decreasing depth). For nodes at the same depth, use node ID as tie breaker (stable). Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented after removal (i.e., the required symbols count remains >=1). Also must never remove root, a required symbol node, or a node with retained children (i.e., if node has children that are retained, it cannot be removed because children depend on parent? Actually in tree, parent pointers. The rule \"never remove a node with retained children\" likely means if a node is removed, its children become orphans? But the problem says \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\". Also \"Never remove... a node with retained children.\" So we must not remove a node if any of its children are retained. But what does \"retained\" mean? The process: initially all nodes are retained. We decide to remove some nodes. The removal order is the actual order in which we mark them removed. The output: removed_node_ids list in order of removal, and retained_node_ids sorted (presumably alphabetically or by ID?). The candidate_artifact says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted. The schema says array of strings, but sorting method not specified. Likely lexicographic sort.\n\nAlso: \"grammar_valid\" - but there's no grammar in the payload? The payload only has nodes and required_symbols. Possibly the grammar is implicit: symbols are defined, but we don't have grammar rules. However, the rule \"grammar_valid\" might be about ensuring that the tree after removal is still valid? But we only have parent pointers; removal of a node would leave its children without parent? Actually, the problem says \"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". But there is no replacement candidate; only deletion. Perhaps the idea is that we can delete a node if it is removable and its symbol is not required, and all required symbols still exist, and no children are retained? Actually \"Never remove a node with retained children\" suggests that if a node has children that will be retained (i.e., not removed), then you cannot remove that node because the children would become disconnected. So to remove a node, all its children must also be removed (or perhaps they are removed first). But the process: consider deeper nodes first, so children are processed before parent. So if a child is removable and we decide to remove it, then the parent may later be considered. If the child is not removable or we choose to retain it, then the parent cannot be removed. So the algorithm: iterate nodes sorted by depth descending, then by node ID ascending (for ties). For each node, if it is removable, not root, its symbol is not required (i.e., not in required_symbols), and it has no children that are retained (i.e., all its children are either already removed or not retained? Actually we need to track which nodes are retained). Initially all nodes are retained. When we remove a node, we add it to removed list. So we need to maintain a set of removed nodes or retained nodes. For a node to be removable, we need that after removal, all required symbols are still present in retained nodes. Also that it has no retained children. That means all its direct children must have been removed (or are not present? but children are present by parent pointer). So the condition is: children must all be removed (i.e., in removed set). So when considering a node, we check if for all child nodes (nodes that have parent == this node's id) those child nodes are already in removed set. If any child is still retained, we cannot remove this node.\n\nAlso \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to check if the required symbols are still present in the set of retained nodes after this removal. That includes the current node's symbol, and any other nodes. So we need to compute counts of each symbol among retained nodes. Before removal, we have counts. If we remove a node, we decrement its symbol count. If any required symbol count becomes 0, we cannot remove.\n\nWe must process in order of depth descending. For ties, node ID (string) ascending. When we decide to remove, we add it to removed list in that order. That gives the removal order.\n\nAt the end, retained_node_ids are the IDs of nodes not removed, sorted.\n\nAlso need to validate: check that all nodes have required fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. But maybe not needed? Problem says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" So in solve, we can assume case is a dict with nodes and required_symbols. nodes is a list of dicts. We should check each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. But we can ignore utility? Not used. Also ensure root parent is null.\n\nWe need to return JSON object with removed_node_ids (list in removal order) and retained_node_ids (sorted list).\n\nEdge cases: If a required symbol is not present at all in the tree? Should raise ValueError? The problem says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case\". Infeasible could be if required_symbols list includes a symbol that doesn't appear in any node. We'll check that at the start.\n\nAlso if root is removable? Root has \"removable\": false, so fine.\n\nImplementation plan:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    # Build maps: id->node dict, child list per parent, symbol counts\n    node_by_id = {}\n    children_of = {}  # parent_id -> list of child node ids\n    symbol_counts = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        node_by_id[nid] = n\n        parent = n[\"parent\"]\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_of.setdefault(parent, []).append(nid)\n        sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n        symbol_counts[sym] = symbol_counts.get(sym, 0) + 1\n    # Validate required symbols exist\n    for sym in required:\n        if sym not in symbol_counts or symbol_counts[sym] == 0:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol '{sym}' not found in tree\")\n    # Initialize retained set with all node ids\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\n    removed = []  # order list\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    # For each node in order:\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n[\"id\"]\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue  # already removed? should not happen because we process once\n        # Check conditions for removal:\n        # 1. Node is removable\n        if not n[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        # 2. Not root (parent is None)\n        if n[\"parent\"] is None:\n            continue\n        # 3. Symbol not required (i.e., not in required set)\n        if n[\"symbol\"] in required:\n            continue\n        # 4. No retained children: all children must be already removed (i.e., not in retained)\n        child_ids = children_of.get(nid, [])\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in child_ids):\n            continue\n        # 5. After removal, all required symbols still present\n        # Temporarily compute new symbol counts: decrement this node's symbol\n        sym = n[\"symbol\"]\n        if symbol_counts[sym] - 1 < 1 and sym in required:\n            # Actually if this symbol is required but we already checked it's not required? No, we checked sym not in required, so this branch won't occur. But what if sym is required but in required set? We already skipped. So fine.\n            pass\n        # But we need to ensure that for all required symbols, count after removal >=1.\n        # Since sym not required, only other required symbols might be affected? No, only this node's symbol is decremented. So as long as sym is not required, other required symbols unchanged. So condition satisfied.\n        # However, what if a required symbol appears only in nodes that are already removed? That could happen if we remove nodes that have required symbol? But we never remove nodes with required symbol because we skip them. So required symbols always have count >=1 throughout. But we need to be safe: could a required symbol appear only in children that are removed? No, children that are removed would not affect count of required symbol if those children have that symbol? Actually if a required symbol appears in a node that is removable (but we don't allow removal because symbol in required), we skip. So required symbols are never removed. Thus condition is automatically satisfied. But to be thorough, we can compute the count after removal and check.\n\n        # Actually we should check: after removal, for each req sym, the count in retained set (excluding this node) should be >=1. Since we never remove required symbol nodes, it's always true. But we can compute it anyway.\n\n        # Determine new count\n        new_counts = symbol_counts.copy()\n        new_counts[sym] -= 1\n        if all(new_counts.get(s, 0) >= 1 for s in required):\n            # Proceed with removal\n            retained.remove(nid)\n            removed.append(nid)\n            # Update symbol_counts\n            symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\n        else:\n            continue\n    # After loop, retained node ids sorted\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nBut note: The condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" ensures structure. Also \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" We already sorted by (-depth, id). Good.\n\nAlso need to consider that when we remove a node, its children should have been removed already because we process deeper first. That's the reason we process deeper first. When we consider a node, all its descendants have been processed already. So children will either be removed or retained. If a child is retained (not removable or we chose to retain it), then parent cannot be removed. That matches.\n\nNow, about the required_symbols check: we needed to ensure that all required symbols are present throughout. Since we never remove nodes whose symbol is required, they remain. But what if a required symbol appears only in a node that is a child of a node we might remove? But we won't remove that child because its symbol is required. So fine.\n\nNow, we must also consider that the root node's symbol is \"root\" and it's required (required_symbols includes \"root\" in fixture). So root stays.\n\nOne more nuance: The fixture shows nodes with utility but we don't use it. The problem description alludes to \"interestingness oracle\" but it's replaced by required_symbols condition. So likely correct.\n\nWe should also validate that all nodes have the required fields. If any missing, raise ValueError.\n\nNow the candidate_artifact says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" We did that.\n\nAlso \"grammar_valid\" - maybe the tree structure must be valid after removal: each retained node's parent must be retained as well? Otherwise orphan? But the rule \"never remove a node with retained children\" ensures that if a parent is removed, its children are also removed. So the remaining tree is a connected subtree from root? Actually, if we remove a node, its children are already removed so no issue. So the retained set forms a rooted tree where all nodes have their parent retained (except root which has no parent). But we didn't check that the parent of a retained node is also retained. However, given the traversal order and the condition that we only remove nodes if all children are already removed, then when we consider a node, its children might be removed, but the node might be retained. Then later when we consider its parent, if any child is retained, parent cannot be removed. So at the end, every retained node's parent (if exists) is also retained because if parent were removed, then that node would have been a child of a removed node, but we only remove parent after children are removed. So if node is retained, its parent must also be retained (since we never remove parent while child is retained). So structure is consistent.\n\nWe should also consider nodes that are not reachable from root? The input is a tree, likely all nodes are descendants of root. But we can assume.\n\nNow, output must have exactly removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. Both arrays of strings. Order for removed is the order we added. For retained, sorted.\n\nWe need to return a JSON serializable dict.\n\nNow, we must encode solve function as a Python source string inside a JSON object with key \"implementation\". The response must be exactly that JSON object. No extra text, no code fences. So we produce:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n\nMake sure to escape newlines and quotes properly.\n\nLet's write the code carefully.\n\nPotential improvements: we need to handle that \"removable\" field may be bool. In fixture it's boolean. Use isinstance? Not needed.\n\nAlso, the candidate_artifact mentions \"registered_candidate_order\" and \"stable_ties\". We implemented stable ties by node ID.\n\nNow, we should also check that required_symbols is a list of strings. If not, raise ValueError.\n\nWe'll implement robust validation.\n\nLet's write the solve function.\n\nTesting with given fixtures:\n\nCase 0: nodes as given. We'll simulate manually? Not needed but trust.\n\nWe can test logic quickly:\n\n- Required symbols: root, s1.\n- Process deeper nodes first: depth 2 nodes: n4 (removable, parent n2, symbol s1) - but s1 is required, so skip. n5 (not removable), n6 (removable, parent n1, symbol s0 - not required). Check children: n6 has no children (or may have but none listed). So it can be removed if all required symbols still present. required symbols: root and s1. s1 appears in root? root has symbol root, n1 has s1, n4 has s1. n6 removal reduces s0 count but not required. So we can remove n6. So removed list: n6. Then depth 1 nodes: n1 (parent root, symbol s1, removable false -> skip), n2 (parent root, symbol s2, removable true, not required). Check children: n4 (child of n2) is still retained because n4 is required? Actually n4's symbol s1 is required, so n4 is retained. Since n4 is a child of n2, n2 has a retained child, so cannot remove n2. n3 (parent root, symbol s0, removable false). n3 has child n5 (not removable, retained). So n3 cannot be removed. Root is not removable. So only n6 removed. Retained: all except n6. Sorted: n1,n2,n3,n4,n5,root. That matches expected? We need to see expected output? Not given but plausible.\n\nCase 1 and 2 similar.\n\nThus code works.\n\nNow output implementation string.\n\nMake sure to indent properly.\n\nWe'll produce the JSON.\n\nBe careful with escaping. Use triple quotes for multiline string? In JSON string, we must escape newlines as \\n and backslashes as \\\\, and double quotes as \\\". So we can manually escape.\n\nI'll write the Python code as a string with proper indentation.\n\nLet's produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"